static struct { int64_t rc, len; char d[335]; } br_s701 = {-1, 334, "# Welcome to brine! Run this program with `brine run`.\n\nfn greet(name: str) -> str:\n    return f\"Hello, {name}!\"\n\nfn main():\n    print(greet(\"brine\"))\n\n    # parallel by default: this runs across all your cores\n    let squares = par [i * i for i in 0..10]\n    print(squares)\n\ntest \"greeting\":\n    assert greet(\"you\") == \"Hello, you!\"\n"};
static struct { int64_t rc, len; char d[2071]; } br_s702 = {-1, 2070, "# AGENTS.md \342\200\224 working on `{name}`\n\nThis is a brine project. brine is a compiled,\nverified, natively parallel language with Python-like syntax.\n\n## Commands\n- `brine check` \342\200\224 type-check and verify (fast; run after every edit)\n- `brine run` \342\200\224 build and run `src/main.br`\n- `brine test` \342\200\224 run all `test \"...\":` blocks\n- `brine fmt` \342\200\224 format all sources (always run before committing)\n- `brine guide` \342\200\224 the full language reference (read it once)\n- `brine explain <CODE>` \342\200\224 explanation of any error code, e.g. `brine explain E0601`\n\nAdd `--json` to any command for machine-readable output (one JSON object per line).\n\n## Rules that commonly trip people up\n- `let` is immutable, `var` is mutable; no shadowing.\n- No implicit numeric conversions: write `float(i)`, `int(x)`.\n- Arguments are passed by value. A function that changes the caller's variable\n  declares `ref x: T` and callers write `f(ref x)`; methods use `ref self`.\n- `requires`/`ensures`/`invariant`/`prove` must be proven at compile time.\n  If the prover can't, the error lists the known facts; add an `invariant`,\n  a `requires`, or an `assert` (runtime check that becomes a known fact).\n- Inside `par for i in ...:` you may only write `out[i]` of outer arrays.\n- Maps: `m[k]` panics if `k` is missing; test with `k in m` or use `m.get(k)`.\n- No negative indexes (`xs[-1]` is an error); write `xs[len(xs) - 1]`.\n- Strings index and slice by bytes; `s[i]` is a one-character `str`.\n- One expression can't change a variable and also read it, or change it twice\n  (`print(xs.pop(), xs)`, `xs.push(xs.pop())`); split it into two statements.\n  Uses on different sides of `and`/`or`/`if-else` are fine.\n- Types are optional (`let x = 5` or `let x: int = 5`); if brine.toml has\n  `typed = true` under `[check]`, every `let`/`var`/`for` variable needs one.\n- `match` must cover every case; read enum variant data with `match`, not `.field`.\n- Expected failures return `Result[T, E]` (`Ok(x)` / `Err(e)`) or `Option[T]`\n  (`Some(x)` / `None`); `panic` is for bugs. `x\?` passes a failure up to the caller.\n"};
